o, its complement summing to 28 does include zero.\n\nTherefore:\n\nNumber of subsets summing to 28:\n\n- 2 subsets include zero (complements of the two subsets summing to 17 without zero)\n\n- 9 subsets do not include zero (complements of the nine subsets summing to 17 with zero)\n\nSimilarly, subsets summing to 17:\n\n- 9 subsets include zero\n\n- 2 subsets do not include zero\n\nTherefore, for case 1 (sum 28):\n\n- 2 subsets include zero\n\n- 9 subsets do not include zero\n\nFor case 2 (sum 17):\n\n- 9 subsets include zero\n\n- 2 subsets do not include zero\n\nNow, we can compute the number of valid permutations for each case.\n\nFirst, case 1: S_odd=28 (sum of odd positions). For each subset of 5 digits summing to 28:\n\n- If the subset includes zero (2 subsets), the number of valid permutations for the odd group is 5! -4! =96 (as earlier)\n\n- If the subset does not include zero (9 subsets), the number of valid permutations is 5! =120\n\nFor each subset, the even group can be arranged freely in 5! ways (since zero is allowed in even positions).\n\nTherefore, total numbers for case 1:\n\n= (number of subsets with zero) * 96 * 5! + (number of subsets without zero) *120 *5!\n\n= 2*96*120 +9*120*120\n\nCompute this:\n\nFirst term: 2*96*120 = 2*11520=23040\n\nSecond term:9*120*120=9*14400=129600\n\nTotal for case 1:23040 +129600=152,640\n\nSimilarly, for case 2: S_odd=17 (sum of odd positions). Now, the subsets summing to 17:\n\n- 9 subsets include zero\n\n- 2 subsets do not include zero\n\nBut wait, for case 2, the odd positions sum to 17. So the subsets for the odd positions are the ones summing to 17. Therefore:\n\n- For subsets summing to 17 that include zero (9 subsets), the odd group includes zero. Therefore, when permuting, we need to ensure that zero is not in the first position (position 1). Therefore, similar to before, the number of valid permutations is 5! -4! =96 for each such subset.\n\n- For subsets summing to 17 that do not include zero (2 subsets), 