positive measure given the conditions \n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 x^m f(x) \\, dx = 0 \\quad \\text{for} \\quad m = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, n-1,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 x^n f(x) \\, dx = 1.\n\\]\n\nWe will use the function \\((x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x)\\) and analyze its integral.\n\n#### Step 1: Calculate the integral of \\((x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x)\\):\n\nGiven the integrals of \\( x^m f(x) \\) where \\( m \\) ranges from \\( 0 \\) to \\( n-1 \\) are all zero, consider the term \\((x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x)\\),\n\nFirst, expand \\((x- \\frac{1}{2})^n\\) using the binomial theorem:\n\n\\[\n(x - \\frac{1}{2})^n = \\sum_{k = 0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} (-\\frac{1}{2})^k.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 (x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x) \\, dx = \\int_0^1 \\left( \\sum_{k = 0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} (-\\frac{1}{2})^k \\right) f(x) \\, dx.\n\\]\n\nSince the integrals \\(\\int_0^1 x^m f(x) \\, dx\\) for all \\(m = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1\\) are zero, only the term where \\( n = k \\) remains:\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 (x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x) \\, dx = \\binom{n}{n} (-\\frac{1}{2})^n \\int_0^1 x^0 f(x) \\, dx = (-\\frac{1}{2})^n \\int_0^1 f(x) \\, dx.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\int_0^1 x^n f(x) \\, dx = 1\\), it follows that\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 (x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x) \\, dx = 1.\n\\]\n\n#### Step 2: Analyze the result:\n\nRecall that\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 (x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x) \\, dx = 1.\n\\]\n\nTo proceed, note that if \\( |f(x)| < 2n(n+1) \\) almost everywhere (except possibly on a set of measure zero), then \n\n\\[\n\\left| \\int_0^1 (x - \\frac{1}{2})^n f(x) \\, dx \\right| \n\\leq 2n(n+1) \\int_0^1 |(x - \\frac{1}{2})^n| \\, dx.\n\\]\n\nEvaluate the integral,\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^1 |(x - \\frac{1}{2})^n| \\, dx = 2 \\int_0^{1/2} (\\frac{1}{2} - x)^n \\, dx\n\\]\n\nPerform a substitution \\( u = \\frac{1}{2} - x \\), then \\(du = -dx\\).\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^{1/2} (\\frac{1}{2} - x)^n \\, dx = \\int_0^{1/2} u^n \\, (-du) = \\int_{1/2}^0 u^n \\, du.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\int_0^{1/2} (\\frac{1}{2} - x)^n \\, 