haps the formula I derived earlier is equivalent to Brahmagupta's formula. Let me see.\n\nBrahmagupta's formula: Area = \u221a[(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)(s - d)]\n\nBut the formula I derived through the trigonometric approach was:\n\nArea = (1/2) * sqrt[ (2(ab + cd))\u00b2 - (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2)^2 ]\n\nLet me compute that expression step by step. Maybe it simplifies to Brahmagupta's formula.\n\nFirst, expand the expression inside the square root:\n\n(2(ab + cd))\u00b2 - (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2)^2\n\nLet me compute this using algebra. Let's denote X = ab + cd, Y = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2\n\nSo, the expression becomes (2X)\u00b2 - Y\u00b2 = 4X\u00b2 - Y\u00b2 = (2X - Y)(2X + Y)\n\nSo, factor it as a difference of squares:\n\n[2(ab + cd) - (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2)][2(ab + cd) + (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2)]\n\nLet me compute each factor separately.\n\nFirst factor: 2(ab + cd) - (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\n= 2ab + 2cd - a\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 + d\u00b2\n\n= (-a\u00b2 + 2ab - b\u00b2) + (c\u00b2 + 2cd + d\u00b2)\n\n= -(a\u00b2 - 2ab + b\u00b2) + (c + d)^2\n\n= -(a - b)^2 + (c + d)^2\n\nSimilarly, second factor: 2(ab + cd) + (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2)\n\n= 2ab + 2cd + a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - c\u00b2 - d\u00b2\n\n= (a\u00b2 + 2ab + b\u00b2) + (-c\u00b2 + 2cd - d\u00b2)\n\n= (a + b)^2 - (c\u00b2 - 2cd + d\u00b2)\n\n= (a + b)^2 - (c - d)^2\n\nTherefore, the entire expression becomes:\n\n[-(a - b)^2 + (c + d)^2][(a + b)^2 - (c - d)^2]\n\nThis is a product of two differences of squares:\n\nFirst term: (c + d)^2 - (a - b)^2 = [c + d + a - b][c + d - a + b]\n\nSecond term: (a + b)^2 - (c - d)^2 = [a + b + c - d][a + b - c + d]\n\nTherefore, the entire expression under the square root becomes:\n\n[c + d + a - b][c + d - a + b][a + b + c - d][a + b - c + d]\n\nLet me rearrange the terms:\n\n[ (a + b + c - d)(a + b - c + d)(a - b + c + d)(-a + b + c + d) ] \n\nWait, but each of these f