.\n\nSuppose we take one x_i slightly larger than \u03c0/4 and another slightly smaller, keeping the sum \u03c0. Let's say x\u2081 = \u03c0/4 + \u03b5, x\u2082 = \u03c0/4 - \u03b5, and x\u2083 = x\u2084 = \u03c0/4. Then check if the product decreases.\n\nCompute the product terms for x\u2081 and x\u2082:\n\nFor x\u2081 = \u03c0/4 + \u03b5, sin\u00b2x\u2081 \u2248 sin\u00b2(\u03c0/4) + 2 sin(\u03c0/4) cos(\u03c0/4) * \u03b5 = 1/2 + (2*(\u221a2/2)*(\u221a2/2))*\u03b5 = 1/2 + (1)*\u03b5. So sin\u00b2x\u2081 \u2248 1/2 + \u03b5. Similarly, sin\u00b2x\u2082 \u2248 1/2 - \u03b5.\n\nCompute 2y + 1/y for y \u2248 1/2 + \u03b5:\n\n\u2248 2*(1/2 + \u03b5) + 1/(1/2 + \u03b5) \u2248 1 + 2\u03b5 + 2 - 4\u03b5 + ... (using 1/(0.5 + \u03b5) \u2248 2 - 4\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2 - ...)\n\nWait, let's do a Taylor expansion. Let y = 1/2 + \u03b5, then 1/y \u2248 2 - 4\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2.\n\nSo 2y + 1/y \u2248 2*(1/2 + \u03b5) + (2 - 4\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2) = 1 + 2\u03b5 + 2 - 4\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2 \u2248 3 - 2\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2.\n\nSimilarly for y = 1/2 - \u03b5:\n\n2y + 1/y \u2248 2*(1/2 - \u03b5) + (2 + 4\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2) = 1 - 2\u03b5 + 2 + 4\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2 \u2248 3 + 2\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2.\n\nMultiplying these two terms:\n\n(3 - 2\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2)(3 + 2\u03b5 + 8\u03b5\u00b2) \u2248 9 + (6\u03b5 + 24\u03b5\u00b2) - (6\u03b5 - 4\u03b5\u00b2) + ... Hmm, expanding:\n\nFirst, 3*3 = 9.\n\n3*(2\u03b5) = 6\u03b5\n\n3*(8\u03b5\u00b2) = 24\u03b5\u00b2\n\n(-2\u03b5)*3 = -6\u03b5\n\n(-2\u03b5)*(2\u03b5) = -4\u03b5\u00b2\n\n(-2\u03b5)*(8\u03b5\u00b2) = -16\u03b5\u00b3\n\nSimilarly, the other terms, but higher order.\n\nSo combining:\n\n9 + (6\u03b5 -6\u03b5) + (24\u03b5\u00b2 -4\u03b5\u00b2) + ... = 9 + 0\u03b5 + 20\u03b5\u00b2 + ... \u2248 9 + 20\u03b5\u00b2.\n\nThe original terms when \u03b5=0 are 3*3=9. So the product of the two perturbed terms is approximately 9 + 20\u03b5\u00b2, which is larger than 9. So the total product becomes (9 + 20\u03b5\u00b2)*3*3 = (