or t_x and t_y. Let me express this as:\n\nLet vector \\( \\vec{OT} = (t_x, t_y) \\), which is a point on the circle, so \\( ||\\vec{OT}|| = b \\).\n\nThe vector \\( \\vec{PT} = (t_x - x\u2080, t_y - y\u2080) \\) must be perpendicular to \\( \\vec{OT} \\), because the radius at T is perpendicular to the tangent line at T. Therefore, the dot product of \\( \\vec{OT} \\) and \\( \\vec{PT} \\) is zero:\n\n\\( \\vec{OT} \\cdot \\vec{PT} = t_x(t_x - x\u2080) + t_y(t_y - y\u2080) = 0 \\)\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\n\\( t_x\u00b2 - t_x x\u2080 + t_y\u00b2 - t_y y\u2080 = 0 \\)\n\nBut since \\( t_x\u00b2 + t_y\u00b2 = b\u00b2 \\), substitute:\n\n\\( b\u00b2 - t_x x\u2080 - t_y y\u2080 = 0 \\)\n\nWhich gives:\n\n\\( t_x x\u2080 + t_y y\u2080 = b\u00b2 \\)\n\nWhich is the same as equation 2 above. Therefore, the tangent points T are the solutions to:\n\n1. \\( t_x\u00b2 + t_y\u00b2 = b\u00b2 \\)\n\n2. \\( t_x x\u2080 + t_y y\u2080 = b\u00b2 \\)\n\nThis is a system of equations. To solve it, we can parameterize T. Let me consider t_x and t_y as variables.\n\nLet me write equation 2 as:\n\n\\( t_x x\u2080 + t_y y\u2080 = b\u00b2 \\)\n\nWe can express t_x from equation 2:\n\n\\( t_x = (b\u00b2 - t_y y\u2080)/x\u2080 \\)\n\nSubstitute into equation 1:\n\n\\( [(b\u00b2 - t_y y\u2080)/x\u2080]^2 + t_y\u00b2 = b\u00b2 \\)\n\nMultiply through by \\( x\u2080\u00b2 \\):\n\n\\( (b\u00b2 - t_y y\u2080)^2 + t_y\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 = b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 \\)\n\nExpand the first term:\n\n\\( b^4 - 2 b\u00b2 t_y y\u2080 + t_y\u00b2 y\u2080\u00b2 + t_y\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 = b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 \\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\( b^4 - 2 b\u00b2 t_y y\u2080 + t_y\u00b2(y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2) = b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 \\)\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n\\( t_y\u00b2(y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2) - 2 b\u00b2 y\u2080 t_y + b^4 - b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 = 0 \\)\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in t_y:\n\nLet me denote coefficients:\n\nA = y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2\n\nB = -2 b\u00b2 y\u2080\n\nC = b^4 - b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2\n\nThus:\n\nA t_y\u00b2 + B t_y + C = 0\n\nSolving for t_y:\n\nt_y = [2 b\u00b2 y\u2080 \u00b1 \u221a(4 b^4 y\u2080\u00b2 - 4 (y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2)(b^4 - b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2))]/(2(y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2))\n\nSimplify discriminant D:\n\nD = 4 b^4 y\u2080\u00b2 - 4 (y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2)(b^4 - b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2)\n\nFactor out 4:\n\nD = 4 [b^4 y\u2080\u00b2 - (y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2)(b^4 - b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2)]\n\nExpand the second term:\n\n= 4 [b^4 y\u2080\u00b2 - (y\u2080\u00b2 b^4 - y\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2 b^4 - x\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2)]\n\n= 4 [b^4 y\u2080\u00b2 - y\u2080\u00b2 b^4 + y\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 - x\u2080\u00b2 b^4 + x\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2]\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n- b^4 y\u2080\u00b2 cancels with - y\u2080\u00b2 b^4\n\nLeft with:\n\n= 4 [y\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 - x\u2080\u00b2 b^4 + x\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2]\n\nFactor out x\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2:\n\n= 4 x\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 [y\u2080\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2]\n\nBut since P(x\u2080, y\u2080) is on the ellipse, \\( x\u2080\u00b2/a\u00b2 + y\u2080\u00b2/b\u00b2 = 1 \\implies y\u2080\u00b2 = b\u00b2(1 - x\u2080\u00b2/a\u00b2) \\). Substitute into the expression inside the brackets:\n\ny\u2080\u00b2 - b\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2 = b\u00b2(1 - x\u2080\u00b2/a\u00b2) - b\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2 = -b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2/a\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2 = x\u2080\u00b2(1 - b\u00b2/a\u00b2) = x\u2080\u00b2 c\u00b2/a\u00b2 (since c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, D = 4 x\u2080\u00b2 b\u00b2 * x\u2080\u00b2 c\u00b2 / a\u00b2 = 4 x\u2080^4 b\u00b2 c\u00b2 / a\u00b2\n\nThus, square root of D is 2 x\u2080\u00b2 b c / a\n\nTherefore, t_y = [2 b\u00b2 y\u2080 \u00b1 2 x\u2080\u00b2 b c / a ] / [2(y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2)]\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator:\n\nt_y = [b\u00b2 y\u2080 \u00b1 x\u2080\u00b2 b c / a ] / (y\u2080\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2)\n\nSimilarly, we can write t_x from equation 2:\n\nt_x = (b\u00b2 - t_y y\u2080)/x\u2080\n\nTherefore, once we have t_y, we can compute t_x.\n\nBut this seems complicated. Maybe there's a better way.\n\nAlternatively, the tangent lines from P(x\u2080, y\u2080) to the circle x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = b\u00b2 can be parametrized using the parametric equations of the circle.\n\nLet me parametrize the circle as \\( T(b \\cos \\theta, b \\sin \\theta) \\). Then, the tangent line at T has equation \\( x \\cos \\theta + y \\sin \\theta = b \\). This line passes through P(x\u2080, y\u2080), so:\n\nx\u2080 cos \u03b8 + y\u2080 sin \u03b8 = b\n\nThis is the equation that relates \u03b8 to the point P. So for a given P, we can solve for \u03b8 such that this equation holds.\n\nTherefore, \u03b8 must satisfy:\n\nx\u2080 cos \u03b8 + y\u2080 sin \u03b8 = b\n\nThis is a linear equation in cos \u03b8 and sin \u03b8. Let's write it as:\n\nA cos \u03b8 + B sin \u03b8 = C\n\nwhere A = x\u2080, B = y\u2080, C = b.\n\nTo solve for \u03b8, we can write this as:\n\nsqrt(A\u00b2 + B\u00b2) cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6) = C\n\nwhere \u03c6 = arctan(B/A) = arctan(y\u2080/x\u2080)\n\nTherefore:\n\ncos(\u03b8 - \u03c6) = C / sqrt(A\u00b2 + B\u00b2) = b / sqrt(x\u2080\u00b2 + y\u2080\u00b2)\n\nBut since P is on the ellipse, x\u2080\u00b2/a\u00b2 + y\u2080\u00b2/b\u00b2 =1, so y\u2080\u00b2 = b\u00b2(1 - x\u2080\u00b2/a\u00b2). Therefore,\n\nsqrt(x\u2080\u00b2 + y\u2080\u00b2) = sqrt(x\u2080\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - (b\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2)/a\u00b2) = sqrt(b\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2(1 - b\u00b2/a\u00b2)) = sqrt(b\u00b2 + x\u2080\u00b2 c\u00b2/a\u00b2)\n\nWhere c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. Therefore,\n\nsqrt(x\u2080\u00b2 + y\u2080\u00b2) = sqrt( b\u00b2 + (x\u2080\u00b2 c\u00b2)/a\u00b2 )\n\nThis complicates the expression. Let's denote D = sqrt(b\u00b2 + (x\u2080\u00b2 c\u00b2)/a\u00b2 )\n\nThus, cos(\u03b8 - \u03c6) = b / D\n\nFor real solutions, we need |b / D| \u2264 1, which is true since D \u2265 b (as x\u2080\u00b2 c\u00b2 /a\u00b2 \u22650). Therefore, \u03b8 exists.\n\nTherefore, \u03b8 = \u03c6 \u00b1 arccos(b / D) + 2\u03c0 n\n\nTherefore, there are two solutions for \u03b8, corresponding to the two tangent lines from P to the circle.\n\nTherefore, the two tangent points T correspond to angles \u03b8\u2081 and \u03b8\u2082.\n\nTherefore, the tangent lines are:\n\nx cos \u03b8\u2081 + y sin \u03b8\u2081 = b\n\nand\n\nx cos \u03b8\u2082 + y sin \u03b8\u2082 = b\n\nEach of these lines passes through P and is tangent to the circle at T\u2081 and T\u2082 respectively.\n\nBut the problem mentions \"a tangent\" intersecting the ellipse again at Q. Therefore, each tangent line will intersect the ellipse at P and another point Q. However, since P is already on the ellipse, and the line is tangent to the circle but not necessarily to the ellipse, the line will intersect the ellipse at P and Q.\n\nWait, but if the line is tangent to the circle, does it necessarily intersect the ellipse at two points? Or could it be tangent to the ellipse as well?\n\nIn general, a line can intersect an ellipse at 0, 1, or 2 points. Since the line passes through P, which is on the ellipse, it must intersect the ellipse at least once. If the line is tangent to the ellipse at P, then it would only intersect at P, but since the line is tangent to the circle, not necessarily to the ellipse. Therefore, unless the line is both tangent to the circle and the ellipse at P, which would require specific conditions, in general, the line will intersect the ellipse at P and another point Q.\n\nTherefore, we need to find the other intersection point Q of the tangent line with the ellipse.\n\nTo find Q, we can parametrize the line and solve for its intersection with the ellipse.\n\nGiven the tangent line equation from point P to the circle: x cos \u03b8 + y sin \u03b8 = b\n\nWe can parameterize this line. Let me express it in terms of a parameter t.\n\nAlternatively, since the line passes through P(x\u2080, y\u2080) and is tangent to the circle at T(b cos \u03b8, b sin \u03b8), we can parametrize the line as:\n\nFrom T to P, direction vector is (x\u2080 - b cos \u03b8, y\u2080 - b sin \u03b8). Therefore, parametric equations:\n\nx = b cos \u03b8 + t(x\u2080 - b cos \u03b8)\n\ny = b sin \u03b8 + t(y\u2080 - b sin \u03b8)\n\nWe know that when t = 0, we are at T, and when t = 1, we are at P. However, since the line extends beyond P, to find the other intersection with the ellipse, we need to find t \u2260 1 such that (x, y) is on the ellipse.\n\nSubstitute x and y into the ellipse equation:\n\n[ b cos \u03b8 + t(x\u2080 - b cos \u03b8) ]\u00b2 / a\u00b2 + [ b sin \u03b8 + t(y\u2080 - b sin \u03b8) ]\u00b2 / b\u00b2 = 1\n\nThis equation needs to be solved for t. We know that t = 1 is a solution (point P). The other solution will give us point Q.\n\nLet me expand this equation:\n\nFirst term:\n\n[ b cos \u03b8 + t(x\u2080 - b cos \u03b8) ]\u00b2 / a\u00b2\n\n= [ b cos \u03b8 (1 - t) + t x\u2080 ]\u00b2 / a\u00b2\n\nSimilarly, second term:\n\n[ b sin \u03b8 + t(y\u2080 - b sin \u03b8) ]\u00b2 / b\u00b2\n\n= [ b sin \u03b8 (1 - t) + t y\u2080 ]\u00b2 / b\u00b2\n\nTherefore, expanding both terms:\n\nFirst term:\n\n[ (b cos \u03b8 (1 - t) + t x\u2080 ) ]\u00b2 / a\u00b2\n\n= [ b\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 (1 - t)\u00b2 + 2 b cos \u03b8 (1 - t) t x\u2080 + t\u00b2 x\u2080\u00b2 ] / a\u00b2\n\nSecond term:\n\n[ (b sin \u03b8 (1 - t) + t y\u2080 ) ]\u00b2 / b\u00b2\n\n= [ b\u00b2 sin\u00b2 \u03b8 (1 - t)\u00b2 + 2 b sin \u03b8 (1 - t) t y\u2080 + t\u00b2 y\u2080\u00b2 ] / b\u00b2\n\n= [ sin\u00b2 \u03b8 (1 - t)\u00b2 + 2 sin \u03b8 (1 - t) t y\u2080 / b + t\u00b2 y\u2080\u00b2 / b\u00b2 ]\n\nSum both terms and set equal to 1:\n\n[ b\u00b2 cos\u00b2 \u03b8 (1 - t)^2 + 2 b cos \u03b8 x\u2080 t (1 - t) + x\u2080\u00b2 t\u00b2 ] / a\u00b2 + [ sin\u00b2 \u03b8 (1 - t)^2 + 2 sin \u03b8 y\u2080 t (1 - t)/b + y\u2080\u00b2 t\u00b2 / b\u00b2 ] = 1\n\nThis equation is quadratic in t. Let's colle