e identity for sine, which is:\n     \\[\n     \\sin(3\\alpha) = 3\\sin(\\alpha) - 4\\sin^3(\\alpha)\n     \\]\n\n4. **Substitution and Simplification**:\n   - Substitute the identity into the area ratio:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{S'}{S} = \\left| \\frac{3\\sin(\\alpha) - 4\\sin^3(\\alpha)}{\\sin(\\alpha)} \\right|\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the expression:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{S'}{S} = \\left| 3 - 4\\sin^2(\\alpha) \\right|\n     \\]\n\n5. **Analysis and Conclusion**:\n   - As \\(\\sin^2(\\alpha)\\) ranges from 0 to 1, evaluate the maximum value of the expression:\n     \\[\n     3 - 4\\sin^2(\\alpha) \n     \\]\n   - The maximum possible value occurs when \\(\\sin^2(\\alpha) = 0\\):\n     \\[\n     3 - 4\\cdot0 = 3\n     \\]\n   - For any other value of \\( \\sin^2(\\alpha) \\) within its range, the expression \\( 3 - 4\\sin^2(\\alpha) \\) will be less than 3.\n   - Hence, for all valid \\(\\alpha\\), it holds:\n     \\[\n     \\left| 3 - 4\\sin^2(\\alpha) \\right| < 3\n     \\]\n\nThus, \\(\\frac{S'}{S}\\) is conclusively proven to be less than 3:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\frac{S'}{S} < 3 }\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_{\\Sigma}\\left(x^{2}+y^{2}\\right) d\\sigma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma$ is the upper hemisphere\n\n$$\nx^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=9, \\quad z \\geq 0\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to calculate the surface integral of x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 over the upper hemisphere of the sphere x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 = 9. Hmm, let me start by recalling what a surface integral is. A surface integral is like a double integral but over a curved surface instead of a flat plane. The formula for a surface integral is \u222b\u222b_\u03a3 f(x,y,z) d\u03c3, and in this case, f(x,y,z) is x\u00b2 + y\u00b2. The surface \u03a3 is the upper hemisphere, which means z is greater than or equal to 0, and the radius of the sphere is 3 because 9 is 3 squared. \n\nFirst, I need to parameterize the surface. Since it's a hemisphere, spherical coordinates might be a good idea. In spherical coordinates, x = \u03c1 sin\u03c6 cos\u03b8, y = \u03c1 sin\u03c6 sin\u03b8, z = \u03c1 cos\u03c6. But since the radius \u03c1 is fixed at 3 (because x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 = 9), we can write x = 3 sin\u03c6 cos\u03b8, y = 3 sin\u03c6 sin\u03b8, z = 3 cos\u03c6. The parameters here would be \u03b8 and \u03c6. For the upper hemisphere, z \u2265 0, so \u03c6 goes from 0 to \u03c0/2 (since \u03c6 is the angle from the positive z-axis), and \u03b8 goes from 0 to 2\u03c0 to cover the full circle around the z-axis.\n\nNext, I need to compute the differential surface element d\u03c3. From what I remember, when parameterizing a surface with two variables, say u and v, the differential surface element is the magnitude of the cross product of the partial derivatives of the position vector with respect to u and v. So in this case, the parameters are \u03b8 and \u03c6. Let's denote the position vector r(\u03b8, \u03c6) = (3 sin\u03c6 cos\u03b8, 3 sin\u03c6 sin\u03b8, 3 cos\u03c6).\n\nTo find d\u03c3, I need to compute the cross product of the partial derivatives of r with respect to \u03b8 and \u03c6. Let's compute those partial derivatives.\n\nFirst, \u2202r/\u2202\u03b8:\nThe derivative of x with respect to \u03b8 is -3 sin\u03c6 sin\u03b8,\nthe derivative of y with respect to \u03b8 is 3 sin\u03c6 cos\u03b8,\nthe derivative of z with respect to \u03b8 is 0.\nSo, \u2202r/\u2202\u03b8 = (-3 sin\u03c6 sin\u03b8, 3 sin\u03c6 cos\u03b8, 0).\n\nNext, \u2202r/\u2202\u03c6:\nThe derivative of x with respect to \u03c6 is 3 cos\u03c6 cos\u03b8,\nthe derivative of y with respect to \u03c6 is 3 cos\u03c6 sin\u03b8,\nthe derivative of z with respect to \u03c6 is -3 sin\u03c6.\nSo, \u2202r/\u2202\u03c6 = (3 cos\u03c6 cos\u03b8, 3 cos\u03c6 sin\u03b8, -3 sin\u03c6).\n\nNow, take the cross product of \u2202r/\u2202\u03b8 and \u2202r/\u2202\u03c6. Let's compute that:\n\n