 -b*q =d. \n\nTherefore, the difference d can be achieved by a sequence of a right jumps and b left jumps. Since the frog's path includes all possible jumps (as it makes multiple cycles of right and left jumps), such a sequence must exist as a segment within the path. \n\nHence, the positions S_i and S_{i + a + b} (where the segment from i to i + a + b consists of a right jumps and b left jumps) will have a difference of d. \n\nTherefore, for any natural number d < p + q, there exist two numbers visited by the frog that differ by exactly d. \n\nThis completes the proof.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that for any natural number \\( d < p + q \\), there exist two numbers visited by the frog that differ by exactly \\( d \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Path Structure**: The frog starts at 0 and returns to 0 after \\( k \\) right jumps (\\( +p \\)) and \\( m \\) left jumps (\\( -q \\)), where \\( kp = mq \\). Since \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are coprime, \\( k = qt \\) and \\( m = pt \\) for some \\( t \\in \\mathbb{N} \\). The total jumps are \\( t(p + q) \\).\n\n2. **Residues Modulo \\( p + q \\)**: Each jump effectively adds \\( p \\) modulo \\( p + q \\) (since \\( -q \\equiv p \\mod (p + q) \\)). The residues of the positions visited modulo \\( p + q \\) cycle through \\( 0, p, 2p, \\ldots, (p + q - 1)p \\), covering all residues uniquely due to the coprimality of \\( p \\) and \\( p + q \\).\n\n3. **B\u00e9zout's Identity**: For any \\( d \\in \\{1, 2, \\ldots, p + q - 1\\} \\), there exist non-negative integers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) such that \\( ap - bq = d \\). This follows from the solvability of the linear Diophantine equation and adjusting solutions using the structure of the frog's path.\n\n4. **Existence of Differences**: Since the frog's path includes all residues modulo \\( p + q \\), and the equation \\( ap - bq = d \\) has non-negative solutions, the segment of the path corresponding to \\( a \\) right jumps and \\( b \\) left jumps achieves the difference \\( d \\).\n\n**Conclusion**: Every natural number \\( d < p + q \\) is realized as the difference between two positions visited by the frog, completing the proof.\n\n\\boxed{For\\ any\\ natural\\ number\\ d < p + q,\\ there\\ exist\\ two\\ numbers\\ visited\\ by\\ the\\ frog\\ that\\ differ\\ by\\ exactly\\ d.}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Case Analysis**: Consider the case when \\( p = q = 1 \\), which is trivial since the frog will always land on even integers, which are 0 distance apart from each other. Now, consider the case when \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are distinct. Assume without loss of generality that \\( p < q \\).\n\n2. **Total Path Length**: \n    - The frog jumps such that the total distance divided by both \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) must be an integer. \n    - Because \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are coprime, the frog's total path length is divisible by \\( \\text{lcm}(p,q) = pq \\). Hence, the total path length is \\( kpq \\) for some integer \\( k \\).\n\n3. **Jump Representation**:\n    - Let \\( a \\) represent the number of short jumps \\( p \\) to the right and \\( b \\) represent the number of long jumps \\( q \\) to the left that the frog makes in one cycle.\n    - Thus, using the linear combination for any integer \\( d \\) which can be written as \\( d = ap - bq \\), we need to find non-negative integers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n\n4. **Diophantine Equation**:\n    - By the properties of the linear combination of coprime integers \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), there exist integers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), such that \\( d = ap - bq \\).\n    - This equation holds trivially since \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) are coprime.\n\n5. **Boundary Conditions**:\n    - We look at all \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) such that the jump series stays within the range \\( 0 \\leq a < q \\) and \\( 0 \\leq b < p \\). \n    - This ensures \\( d \\) stays positive and less than \\( p + q \\) by construction and coprimality.\n\n6. **Window Analysis**:\n    - Define a \"window\" as a series of \\( a + b \\) consecutive jumps. By the properties of \\( p \\) and \\( q \\), analyzing each window reveals the frog\u2019s position mod \\( p+q \\).\n    - By pigeonhole principle, each window wraps modulo \\( p+q \\) ensuring all residues mod \\( p+q \\) are encountered.\n\n7. **Finding Jump Difference**:\n    - For any natural \\( d < p + q \\), since \\( d = ap - bq \\), some position is \\( a \\) short jumps right to one point and \\( b \\) long jumps left to another. \n    - Translates to finding frog's distinct \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) positions \\( d \\) apart where \\( m = rp - sq \\), and \\( n \\) are within \\( p+q \\).\n\n8. **Conclusion**:\n    - Hence, the frog visits two numbers differing by any integer \\( d < p+q \\).\n\nThus, the given problem is proven and the conclusion we reach is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{For any natural } d < p + q, the frog visits two distinct integers differing exactly by } d. \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Determine which of the following fractions is larger with as few calculations as possible:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{33333333331}{33333333334}, \\quad \\frac{22222222221}{22222222223}\n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out which of these two fractions is larger without doing a lot of calculations. The fractions are 33333333331/33333333334 and 22222222221/22222222223. Hmm, both numerators are just a little bit less than the denominators. Let me think about how to compare them.\n\nFirst, maybe I can look at the difference between the numerator and the denominator for each fraction. For the first fraction, 33333333334 minus 33333333331 is 3. For the second fraction, 22222222223 minus 22222222221 is 2. So, the first fraction is 3 less than the denominator, and the second is 2 less. But since the denominators are different sizes, I can't just directly compare those differences. Wait, maybe there's a way to relate them.\n\nAlternatively, I could consider each fraction as 1 minus some small value over the denominator. Let's see:\n\nFor the first fraction: 33333333331/33333333334 = 1 - 3/33333333334\n\nFor the second fraction: 22222222221/22222222223 = 1 - 2/22222222223\n\nSo, comparing these two fractions is the same as comparing which of 3/33333333334 or 2/22222222223 is smaller, because subtracting a smaller number from 1 would result in a larger fraction.\n\nSo, if 3/33333333334 is smaller than 2/22222222223, then the first fraction is larger. Otherwise, the second one is.\n\nSo let's compare 3/33333333334 and 2/22222222223.\n\nCross-multiplying to compare the two fractions: 3*22222222223 vs. 2*33333333334\n\nIf the left side is larger, then 3/33333333334 is larger, making the first original fraction smaller. If the left side is smaller, then 3/33333333334 is smaller, making the first original fraction larger.\n\nCalculate 3*22222222223. Let's see: 22222222223 * 3. Let's compute 22222222223*3. 22,222,222,223 * 3. 22*3=66, then 222*3=666, but maybe there's a pattern here.\n\n22222222223 is 22,222,222,223. So multiplying by 3:\n\n3 * 22,222,222,223 = 66,666,666,669\n\nSimilarly, 2*33333333334. 33,333,333,334 * 2 = 66,666,666,668\n\nSo, 66,666,666,669 vs. 66,666,666,668. The left side is 1 more than the right side. Therefore, 3*22222222223 is greater than 2*33333333334.\n\nTherefore, 3/33333333334 > 2/22222222223.\n\nWait, but that would mean that the amount subtracted from 1 in the first fraction is larger than the amount subtracted in the second fraction. Therefore, 1 - 3/33333333334 < 1 - 2/22222222223. So the second fraction is larger. But wait, that contradicts my initial thought. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let's step back. If 3/33333333334 is greater than 2/22222222223, then subtracting a greater number from 1 would result in a smaller value. Therefore, the first fraction (which is 1 - 3/33333333334) would be smaller than the second fraction (which is 1 - 2/22222222223). Therefore, the second fraction is larger.\n\nBut wait, the cross-multiplication: when comparing 3/33333333334 vs. 2/22222222223, cross-multiplying gives 3*22222222223 vs. 2*33333333334. As we saw, 3*22222222223 = 66,666,666,669 and 2*33333333334 = 66,666,666,668. Since 66,666,666,669 > 66,666,666,668, that means 3/33333333334 > 2/22222222223. Therefore, since the first fraction subtracts a larger value (3/33333333334) from 1, it is smaller than the second fraction, which subtracts a smaller value (2/22222222223) from 1. So 22222222221/22222222223 is larger.\n\nWait, but let me verify this with another approach to be sure. Maybe looking at the decimal approximations. Let's see.\n\nFor the first fraction: 33333333331 / 33333333334. Since both numerator and denominator are very close, let's approximate. Let me subtract numerator from denominator: 33333333334 - 33333333331 = 3. So the fraction is 1 - 3/33333333334. Similarly, the second fraction is 1 - 2/22222222223.\n\nSo, 3/33333333334 = 3 / (3.3333333334 x 10^10) \u2248 9 x 10^-11\n\nWait, 3 divided by 3.3333333334 x 10^10 is 3 / (10/3 x 10^10) since 3.3333333333 is 10/3. Wait, 3.3333333333 x 10^10 is (10/3) x 10^10? Wait, 3.3333333333 is 10/3, so 10/3 x 10^10 is 10^11 / 3. So 3 divided by (10^11 /3) is 9 / 10^11 = 9e-11.\n\nSimilarly, 2/22222222223. 22222222223 is approximately 2.2222222223 x 10^10, which is 20/9 x 10^10. So 2 divided by (20/9 x 10^10) is (2 *9)/(20 x 10^10) = 18 / (20 x 10^10) = 9 / (10 x 10^10) = 9e-11. Wait, that's the same as the first one?\n\nWait, but that can't be right. Because 3 divided by 33333333334 is equal to 3 / (10^10 / 3 + ...) Hmm, maybe my approximations are too rough.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 3/33333333334. Let's note that 33333333334 is 33333333333 +1, which is (10^10)/3 approximately. Wait, 10^10 is 10,000,000,000. 10,000,000,000 divided by 3 is approximately 3,333,333,333.333. So 3,333,333,333.333 is 10^10 / 3.\n\nBut the denominator here is 33,333,333,334, which is 10 times that, so 33,333,333,334 is 10*(3,333,333,333.4). So 3 divided by 33,333,333,334 is 3/(33,333,333,334) = 1/(11,111,111,111.333...)\n\nSimilarly, 2 divided by 22,222,222,223 is 2/(22,222,222,223) \u2248 1/(11,111,111,111.5)\n\nSo comparing 1/11,111,111,111.333... vs. 1/11,111,111,111.5. Since 11,111,111,111.333... is smaller than 11,111,111,111.5, the reciprocal would be larger. So 1/11,111,111,111.333... is larger than 1/11,111,111,111.5, which means 3/33,333,333,334 is larger than 2/22,222,222,223. So that confirms again that 3/33,333,333,334 > 2/22,222,222,223, which implies that 1 - 3/33,333,333,334 < 1 - 2/22,222,222,223, so the second fraction is larger.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 22222222221/22222222223 is the larger fraction.\n\nWait, but let me check with decimal approximations for more concrete numbers.\n\nTake the first fraction: 33333333331 / 33333333334. Let's approximate this. Let's say both are approximately 33,333,333,331 /33,333,333,334 \u2248 0.99999999991. Because 33,333,333,331 is 33,333,333,334 minus 3, so the fraction is 1 - 3/33,333,333,334 \u2248 1 - 0.00000000009 \u2248 0.99999999991.\n\nSimilarly, the second fraction is 22,222,222,221 /22,222,222,223 \u2248 1 - 2/22,222,222,223 \u2248 1 - 0.00000000009 \u2248 0.99999999991. Wait, but that seems the same, but wait, the denominators are different. Let me compute the exact decimal.\n\nWait, 3 /33,333,333,334. 33,333,333,334 is 3.3333333334 x 10^10. So 3 divided by that is 3 / (3.3333333334 x 10^10) = 0.9 / 10^10 = 9 x 10^-11. Similarly, 2 /22,222,222,223 is 2 / (2.2222222223 x 10^10) = 0.9 x 10^-10? Wait, 2 / 2.2222222223 x 10^10. Let's compute 2 / 2.2222222223.\n\n2 divided by 2.2222222223 is approximately 0.9. So 0.9 /10^10 = 9 x 10^-11. So both 3/33,333,333,334 and 2/22,222,222,223 are approximately 9 x10^-11. Hmm, so maybe they are almost the same? But when we cross-multiplied earlier, we saw that 3*22222222223 was slightly larger than 2*33333333334, meaning that 3/33333333334 is slightly larger than 2/22222222223. Therefore, subtracting a slightly larger number from 1 gives a slightly smaller fraction. So the first fraction is slightly smaller.\n\nSo even though the differences are very small, the second fraction is larger.\n\nAnother approach: consider the fractions as (A - 3)/A and (B - 2)/B where A = 33333333334 and B = 22222222223.\n\nSo, comparing (A-3)/A vs. (B-2)/B.\n\nWhich is equivalent to 1 - 3/A vs. 1 - 2/B.\n\nTherefore, the comparison is between 3/A and 2/B. If 3/A > 2/B, then 1 - 3/A < 1 - 2/B, so the second fraction is larger. So we need to check if 3/A > 2/B.\n\nCross multiply: 3B vs. 2A. If 3B > 2A, then 3/A > 2/B.\n\nCompute 3B: 3 * 22222222223 = 66666666669\n\nCompute 2A: 2 * 33333333334 = 66666666668\n\n66666666669 > 66666666668, so 3B > 2A, hence 3/A > 2/B. Therefore, 1 - 3/A < 1 - 2/B. Therefore, the second fraction is larger.\n\nSo, after verifying through multiple methods\u2014cross multiplication, decimal approximation, and algebraic manipulation\u2014it's clear that 22222222221/22222222223 is the larger fraction.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine which fraction is larger between \\(\\frac{33333333331}{33333333334}\\) and \\(\\frac{22222222221}{22222222223}\\) with minimal calculations, we analyze their proximity to 1. Each fraction can be expressed as \\(1 - \\frac{\\text{difference}}{\\text{denominator}}\\):\n\n1. **First Fraction**:  \n   \\(\\frac{33333333331}{33333333334} = 1 - \\frac{3}{33333333334}\\).  \n2. **Second Fraction**:  \n   \\(\\frac{22222222221}{22222222223} = 1 - \\frac{2}{22222222223}\\).\n\nTo compare these, we determine which subtracted term (\\(\\frac{3}{33333333334}\\) or \\(\\frac{2}{22222222223}\\)) is smaller. Cross-multiplying to compare:  \n\\[\n3 \\times 22222222223 \\quad \\text{vs.} \\quad 2 \\times 33333333334.\n\\]\n\nCalculating:  \n- \\(3 \\times 22222222223 = 66666666669\\),  \n- \\(2 \\times 33333333334 = 66666666668\\).\n\nSince \\(66666666669 > 66666666668\\), it follows that:  \n\\[\n\\frac{3}{33333333334} > \\frac{2}{22222222223}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the first fraction subtracts a larger value from 1, making it smaller. Therefore, **\\(\\frac{22222222221}{22222222223}\\) is the larger fraction**.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{22222222221}{22222222223}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo determine which of the following fractions is larger:\n\\[\n\\frac{33333333331}{33333333334} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\frac{22222222221}{22222222223}.\n\\]\n\nWe can proceed with the following steps.\n\n#### I. Method:\n1. Simplify the first fraction:\n   \\[\n   t_{1} = \\frac{33333333331}{33333333334} = \\frac{33333333334 - 3}{33333333334} = 1 - \\frac{3}{33333333334} = 1 - \\frac{1}{11111111111 + \\frac{1}{3}}\n   \\]\n   \n2. Simplify the second fraction:\n   \\[\n   t_{2} = \\frac{22222222221}{22222222223} = \\frac{22222222223 - 2}{22222222223} = 1 - \\frac{2}{22222222223} = 1 - \\frac{1}{11111111111 + \\frac{1}{2}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Compare the subtracted terms:\n   - For \\( t_1 \\), the subtraction term is \\( \\frac{1}{11111111111 + \\frac{1}{3}} \\)\n   - For \\( t_2 \\), the subtraction term is \\( \\frac{1}{11111111111 + \\frac{1}{2}} \\)\n   \n4. Since \\( 11111111111 + \\frac{1}{2} > 11111111111 + \\frac{1}{3} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{11111111111 + \\frac{1}{2}} < \\frac{1}{11111111111 + \\frac{1}{3}}\n   \\]\n\nHence, the second fraction \\( t_{2} \\) is larger than the first fraction \\( t_{1} \\).\n\n#### II. Method:\n1. Scale up the fractions as follows:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{33333333331 \\times 2}{33333333334 \\times 2} = \\frac{66666666662}{66666666668}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{22222222221 \\times 3}{22222222223 \\times 3} = \\frac{66666666663}{66666666669}\n   \\]\n\n2. Now compare the scaled fractions:\n   - The first fraction becomes \\( \\frac{66666666662}{66666666668} \\)\n   - The second fraction becomes \\( \\frac{66666666663}{66666666669} \\)\n\n3. If both numerator and denominator of a fraction, say \\( \\frac{a}{b} \\), are increased by 1, the resulting fraction is larger if \\( \\frac{a}{b} \\) is less than 1, and smaller if \\( \\frac{a}{b} \\) is greater than 1.\n\n4. Since our original fractions are positive proper fractions:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a+1}{b+1} > \\frac{a}{b}\n   \\]\n\nThus, \\( \\frac{66666666663}{66666666669} > \\frac{66666666662}{66666666668} \\).\n\nTherefore, the second fraction \\( \\frac{22222222221}{22222222223} \\) is larger. \n\n#### III. Method:\n1. Rewrite the fractions using a common variable \\( c = 11111111111 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{33333333331}{33333333334} = \\frac{3c - 2}{3c + 1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{22222222221}{22222222223} = \\frac{2c - 1}{2c + 1}\n   \\]\n\n2. Bring the fractions to a common denominator:\n   - For fraction \\( \\frac{3c-2}{3c+1} \\) and \\( \\frac{2c-1}{2c+1} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(3c-2) \\times (2c+1)}{(3c+1) \\times (2c+1)} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{(2c-1) \\times (3c+1)}{(2c+1) \\times (3c+1)}\n   \\]\n\n3. Compute the numerators of the common denominator expressions:\n   \\[\n   (3c - 2)(2c + 1) = 6c^2 - 4c + 3c - 2 = 6c^2 - c - 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (2c - 1)(3c + 1) = 6c^2 + 2c - 3c - 1 = 6c^2 - c - 1\n   \\]\n\n4. Compare the numerators:\n   \\[\n   6c^2 - c - 1 > 6c^2 - c - 2\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the second fraction \\( \\frac{22222222221}{22222222223} \\) is larger.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{22222222221}{22222222223}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Calculate the value of the function:\n\n1) \\( f(x) = \\sqrt{x^2 - 5x + 4} \\)\n\n   a) when \\( x = 0 \\); b) when \\( x = a + 1 \\);\n\n2) \\( \\varphi(x) = 2 \\arcsin x + \\operatorname{arctg} 2x \\) when \\( x = -\\frac{1}{2} \\);\n\n3) \\( y = x^2 \\arccos \\frac{x}{2} - 3x \\operatorname{arcctg} x \\) when \\( x = -1 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to work through these problems step by step. I need to calculate the value of each function at the specified 