rrives before Tourist 2.\n\nWe need to find v.\n\nLet me try to structure this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's figure out the time t1 when Tourist 2 is 5 km ahead of Tourist 1.\n\nUp to time t1, Tourist 1 has been walking at 6 km/h, so distance covered is 6*t1.\n\nTourist 2 has been cycling at v km/h, so distance covered is v*t1.\n\nAt that time, Tourist 2 is 5 km ahead, so:\n\nv*t1 = 6*t1 + 5\n\nSo, v*t1 - 6*t1 = 5\n\nt1*(v - 6) = 5\n\nThus, t1 = 5 / (v - 6)\n\nOkay, that gives us t1 in terms of v.\n\nThen, after t1, Tourist 1 is in the car going 24 km/h. The remaining time until catching up is t2 = 2 - t1 hours (since total time from start is 2 hours when they meet).\n\nSo, from t1 to 2 hours, Tourist 1 travels at 24 km/h, and Tourist 2 continues at v km/h.\n\nAt the time of catching up (t=2), the distances covered by both should be equal.\n\nWait, but Tourist 1 had a head start? Wait no, because Tourist 2 was 5 km ahead at time t1. Then, after t1, Tourist 1 starts moving faster (24 km/h vs v km/h). So, the distance between them at t1 is 5 km (Tourist 2 ahead). Then, Tourist 1 needs to cover that 5 km gap plus whatever distance Tourist 2 covers in the time t2.\n\nWait, no. Wait, at time t1, Tourist 2 is 5 km ahead. Then, from t1 to t=2, Tourist 1 is moving at 24 km/h, Tourist 2 at v km/h. The relative speed is (24 - v) km/h. The distance to cover is 5 km. So, the time taken to catch up is 5 / (24 - v) hours. But this time must be equal to t2 = 2 - t1.\n\nTherefore:\n\nt2 = 5 / (24 - v)\n\nBut t2 = 2 - t1 = 2 - [5 / (v - 6)]\n\nSo,\n\n2 - [5 / (v - 6)] = 5 / (24 - v)\n\nThat's an equation in terms of v. Let's solve this.\n\nLet me write:\n\n2 - (5)/(v - 6) = 5/(24 - v)\n\nMultiply both sides by (v - 6)(24 - v) to eliminate denominators:\n\n2*(v - 6)(24 - v) - 5*(24 - v) = 5*(v - 6)\n\nExpand each term:\n\nFirst term: 2*(v - 6)(24 - v)\n\nLet me expand (v - 6)(24 - v) = v*24 - v^2 - 6*24 + 6v = 24v - v^2 - 144 + 6v = -v^2 + 30v - 144\n\nMultiply by 2: -2v^2 + 60v - 28