 according to our calculations, G(4)=1, which is non-zero, so first player can win.\n\nBut let's check n=6 to see if there's a losing position.\n\nn=6:\n\nThis is getting complex, but let's attempt it.\n\nPossible moves:\n\nRemove 1 pin:\n\n- Removing an end pin (1 or 6): leaves 5 pins (2-3-4-5-6 or 1-2-3-4-5), which we haven't computed yet. Wait, G(5)=4.\n\n- Removing pin 2: leaves 1,3-4-5-6. Components: 1 (G=1) and 3-4-5-6 (adjacency as original). For 3-4-5-6, it's a line of four pins. We have G(4)=1. So the Grundy number is 1 XOR 1 = 0.\n\n- Similarly, removing pin 5: leaves 1-2-3-4 and 6. Grundy number G(4) XOR G(1)=1 XOR 1=0.\n\n- Removing pin 3: leaves 1-2,4-5-6. G(2) XOR G(3)=2 XOR 3=1.\n\n- Removing pin 4: leaves 1-2-3,5-6. G(3) XOR G(2)=3 XOR 2=1.\n\nRemove two adjacent pins:\n\n- Remove 1-2: leaves 3-4-5-6 (G=1 for n=4)\n\n- Remove 2-3: leaves 1,4-5-6 (G=1 XOR G(3)=1 XOR 3=2)\n\n- Remove 3-4: leaves 1-2,5-6 (G=2 XOR 2=0)\n\n- Remove 4-5: leaves 1-2-3,6 (G=3 XOR 1=2)\n\n- Remove 5-6: leaves 1-2-3-4 (G=1)\n\nSo the reachable Grundy numbers from removing two adjacent pins include 1, 2, 0, 2, 1.\n\nSo combining all possible moves:\n\nFrom removing 1 pin: G=4 (removing end), 0 (removing pin 2 or 5), 1 (removing pin 3 or 4)\n\nFrom removing two adjacent pins: G=1, 2, 0\n\nTherefore, the set of Grundy numbers reachable is {0,1,2,4}. The mex is 3. Therefore, G(6)=3.\n\nSo non-zero, first player can win.\n\nContinuing this pattern, it seems that all positions have non-zero Grundy numbers. If that's the case, then the first player can always win by moving to a position with G=0. But wait, in our calculation for n=4, G=1, which is non-zero, but the first player can move to G=0 by removing two middle pins. Similarly, for n=3, G=3, first player can move to G=0.\n\nIf this pattern continues, then no matter the number of pins, the first player can always make a move to a position with G=0, forcing the second player into a losing position. Therefore, the first player can always win.\n\nBut why is this the case? It seems that in this variant of Kayles with static adjacency, the Grundy number is always non-zero for any n\u22651. Therefore, the first player can always win.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a general strategy. For example, in even-length lines, the first player can mirror the second player's moves. But with static adjacency, mirroring might not work because the adjacency is fixed.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the first player can always take the middle pin(s) to split the line into symmetric halves, forcing the second player into a mirrored position. But with static adjacency, splitting the line doesn't create new adjacencies, so mirroring might still be possible.\n\nFor example, in a line of even length, say 4 pins: 1-2-3-4. The first player can remove pins 2 and 3, leaving pins 1 and 4. The second player is forced to take one of them, and the first player takes the remaining one.\n\nFor odd length, say 5 pins: 1-2-3-4-5. The first player can remove the middle pin (3), leaving 1-2 and 4-5. These are two separate pairs. Whatever the second player does in one pair, the first player mirrors in the other pair.\n\nBut in static adjacency, after removing pin 3, the remaining pins are 1-2 and 4-5. Since adjacency is fixed, 1-2 are adjacent, 4-5 are adjacent. So the first player can mirror moves. For example, if the second player takes pin 1, the first player takes pin 5. If the second player takes pins 4-5, the first player takes pins 1-2, etc.\n\nThis strategy ensures that the first player can always make the last move. Similarly, for any odd number of pins, removing the middle pin(s) splits the line into symmetric halves, allowing the first player to mirror.\n\nSimilarly, for even numbers of pins, the first player can remove the two middle pins, splitting the line into two equal halves. Then mirror the second player's moves.\n\nHowever, this requires that the number of pins is even or odd. Let's test this strategy.\n\nCase n even: say n=4. First player removes pins 2 and 3, leaving 1 and 4. Second player has to take one of them, say 1. First player takes 4 and wins. If n=6, first player removes pins 3 and 4, leaving 1-2 and 5-6. Whatever the second player does in one pair, the first player mirrors in the other. For example, if second player takes 1, first player takes 5; if second player takes 1-2, first player takes 5-6, etc. This ensures the first player makes the last move.\n\nCase n odd: n=5. First player removes pin 3, leaving 1-2 and 4-5. Mirroring as above. If n=7, first player removes pin 4, leaving 1-2-3 and 5-6-7. Then mirror the second player's moves. For example, if the second player takes a pin from the left group, the first player takes the corresponding pin from the right group.\n\nThis mirroring strategy works because the two groups are symmetric, and each move in one group can be mirrored in the other. Since the second player is forced to make the first move in one of the groups, the first player can always respond in the other group, eventually making the last move.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the number of pins, the first player can win by initially splitting the line into two symmetric halves (removing the middle pin or two middle pins) and then mirroring the second player's moves. This ensures that the first player always has a move to make after the second player, ultimately making the last move and winning.\n\nTherefore, the key insight is that the first player can always create symmetry, and then mirror the second player's moves. This strategy works because the adjacency is fixed, so the remaining pins don't form new adjacencies, preserving the symmetric structure. Thus, the first player can always force a win.", "deepseek_solution": "To demonstrate that the first player can always win in Kayles with static adjacency (original lineup determines adjacency), we employ a strategy based on symmetry and mirroring:\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Initial Move for Symmetry:**\n   - **Even Number of Pins (n even):** The first player removes the two middle pins. This splits the line into two equal, symmetric halves.\n   - **Odd Number of Pins (n odd):** The first player removes the single middle pin. This splits the line into two symmetric halves.\n\n2. **Mirroring Strategy:**\n   - After splitting the pins symmetrically, whatever move the second player makes on one side, the first player mirrors it on the opposite side. For example:\n     - If the second player knocks down one pin on the left, the first player knocks down the corresponding pin on the right.\n     - If the second player knocks down two adjacent pins on one side, the first player does the same on the other side.\n\n3. **Ensuring the Last Move:**\n   - By maintaining symmetry through mirroring, the first player ensures that they always have a move after the second player. This forces the second player to eventually make the last available move on one side, allowing the first player to mirror and take the last pin(s) on the other side.\n\n**Example Scenarios:**\n\n- **n = 4 (Even):**\n  - First player removes pins 2 and 3, leaving pins 1 and 4.\n  - Second player must take either 1 or 4. First player takes the remaining pin and wins.\n\n- **n = 5 (Odd):**\n  - First player removes pin 3, leaving pairs 1-2 and 4-5.\n  - If the second player takes pin 1, first player takes pin 5. If second player takes 4-5, first player takes 1-2. This continues until the first player makes the last move.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nBy initially creating symmetry and then mirroring all subsequent moves, the first player can always force the second player into a position where they cannot avoid the first player making the last move. Thus, the first player has a guaranteed winning strategy regardless of the second player's actions.\n\n$\\boxed{\\text{The first player can always win by creating symmetry and mirroring the second player's moves.}}$", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo show that the first player can always win in the game of Kayles, we will consider two cases based on the parity (odd or even) of the number of pins. The strategy involves mirroring the moves of the second player after the first player's initial move. Let's detail the steps:\n\n1. **Case 1: Odd number of pins**\n\n   - Suppose the total number of pins, \\( n \\), is odd.\n   - The first player knocks over the middle pin. The position of the middle pin is given by \\( \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rceil \\).\n\n   For example, if there are 5 pins (which is odd):\n   \n   \\[\n   1\\ 2\\ 3\\ 4\\ 5\n   \\]\n\n   The first player knocks over the 3rd pin:\n\n   \\[\n   1\\ 2\\ \\_ \\ 4 \\ 5\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 2: Even number of pins**\n\n   - Suppose the total number of pins, \\( n \\), is even.\n   - The first player knocks over the two middle pins. The positions of the two middle pins are \\( \\frac{n}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{n}{2} + 1 \\).\n   \n   For example, if there are 6 pins (which is even):\n   \n   \\[\n   1\\ 2\\ 3\\ 4\\ 5\\ 6\n   \\]\n\n   The first player knocks over the 3rd and 4th pins:\n\n   \\[\n   1 \\ 2\\ \\_ \\ \\_ \\ 5 \\ 6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Mirroring strategy**\n\n   - After the first player's initial move, the remaining pins will be in two symmetrical sections (left and right).\n   - The strategy for the first player is now to mirror the moves of the second player. If the second player knocks over a pin or two adjacent pins on one side, the first player makes a move that exactly mirrors it on the other side. \n   \n   This strategy works because it ensures that the first player always has a move until the very end. The symmetrical removal of pins guarantees that the first player can always respond to any move made by the second player by making a corresponding move on the mirrored side.\n   \n   - For instance, if after the first player's initial move, the second player knocks over pin 1 on the left section, then the first player knocks over the corresponding pin in the mirrored position (which is the last pin on the right section).\n\n By maintaining this mirroring strategy, the first player forces the second player to make the last move in each symmetrical section. Since the game ends when the last pin(s) are knocked over, the second player is forced to knock over pins in the final move and thus loses.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe strategy of removing the middle pin(s) initially and then mirroring the second player's moves guarantees the first player always has a winning strategy.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The first player can always win.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Solve the linear system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\n-x_{1}-2 x_{2}-6 x_{3}+3 x_{4} & =-1 \\\\\n2 x_{1}+5 x_{2}+14 x_{3}-7 x_{4} & =3 \\\\\n3 x_{1}+7 x_{2}+20 x_{3}-10 x_{4} & =4 \\\\\n-x_{2}-2 x_{3}+x_{4} & =-1\n\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this system of linear equations to solve. Let me write them down again to make sure I have them right:\n\n1. -x\u2081 - 2x\u2082 - 6x\u2083 + 3x\u2084 = -1\n2. 2x\u2081 + 5x\u2082 +14x\u2083 -7x\u2084 = 3\n3. 3x\u2081 +7x\u2082 +20x\u2083 -10x\u2084 =4\n4. -x\u2082 -2x\u2083 +x\u2084 = -1\n\nHmm, four equations with four variables. That means it's a square system, so it might have a unique solution, or maybe no solution or infinitely many if there's dependency. Let me try to solve it step by step. Probably using Gaussian elimination? Let's see.\n\nFirst, maybe write the augmented matrix for this system. That might make it easier to handle.\n\nSo the coefficients matrix would be:\n\nRow 1: -1  -2  -6   3 | -1\n\nRow 2:  2   5  14  -7 | 3\n\nRow 3:  3   7  20 -10 |4\n\nRow 4:  0  -1  -2   1 | -1\n\nWait, the last equation is -x\u2082 -2x\u2083 +x\u2084 = -1, so coefficients are 0 for x\u2081, -1 for x\u2082, -2 for x\u2083, 1 for x\u2084, right? And the constant term is -1.\n\nOkay, so the augmente