u00b2 -12x -2) - (3x\u00b3 -3x\u00b2 -6x) = (0x\u00b3) + ( -x\u00b2 + 3x\u00b2 ) + ( -12x +6x ) + (-2) = 2x\u00b2 -6x -2.\n\nSo now, the remainder is 2x\u00b2 -6x -2, and the divisor is still x\u00b3 -x\u00b2 -2x. Since the degree of the remainder (2) is less than the degree of the divisor (3), we stop here. Therefore, the integrand can be written as:\n\n3 + (2x\u00b2 -6x -2)/[x(x+1)(x-2)].\n\nSo the integral becomes \u222b3 dx + \u222b(2x\u00b2 -6x -2)/[x(x+1)(x-2)] dx.\n\nThe first integral is straightforward: 3x + C. The second integral is the one we need to compute using partial fractions. Let me focus on that part: (2x\u00b2 -6x -2)/[x(x+1)(x-2)].\n\nLet me set up the partial fraction decomposition. The denominator is already factored into linear terms. So we can write:\n\n(2x\u00b2 -6x -2)/[x(x+1)(x-2)] = A/x + B/(x+1) + C/(x-2)\n\nOur goal is to find constants A, B, and C such that:\n\n2x\u00b2 -6x -2 = A(x+1)(x-2) + Bx(x-2) + Cx(x+1)\n\nLet me expand the right-hand side:\n\nFirst, expand each term:\n\nA(x+1)(x-2) = A(x\u00b2 -2x +x -2) = A(x\u00b2 -x -2)\n\nBx(x-2) = B(x\u00b2 -2x)\n\nCx(x+1) = C(x\u00b2 +x)\n\nTherefore, combining all terms:\n\nA(x\u00b2 -x -2) + B(x\u00b2 -2x) + C(x\u00b2 +x) = (A + B + C)x\u00b2 + (-A -2B + C)x + (-2A)\n\nSet this equal to the left-hand side: 2x\u00b2 -6x -2. Therefore, we can equate coefficients for each power of x:\n\nFor x\u00b2: A + B + C = 2\n\nFor x: -A -2B + C = -6\n\nFor constant term: -2A = -2\n\nSo, let's solve these equations step by step.\n\nFrom the constant term: -2A = -2 \u21d2 A = 1.\n\nSubstitute A = 1 into the first equation: 1 + B + C = 2 \u21d2 B + C = 1.\n\nSubstitute A = 1 into the second equation: -1 -2B + C = -6 \u21d2 -2B + C = -5.\n\nNow we have two equations:\n\n1) B + C = 1\n\n2) -2B + C = -5\n\nLet me subtract equation 1 from equation 2:\n\n(-2B + C) - (B + C) = -5 -1 \u21d2 -3B = -6 \u21d2 B = 2.\n\nThen, substitute B = 2 into equation 1: 2 + C = 1 \u21d2 C = -1.\n\nSo, A = 1, B = 2, C = -1.\n\nTherefore, the partial fractions decomposition is:\n\n(2x\u00b2 -6x -2)/[x(x+1)(x-2)] = 1/x + 2/(x+1) -1/(x-2)\n\nSo now, the integral becomes:\n\n\u222b3 dx + \u222b[1/x + 2/(x+1) -1/(x-2)] dx\n\nIntegrate term by term:\n\nFirst integral: 3x\n\nSecond integral:\n\n\u222b1/x dx = ln|x|\n\n\u222b2/(x+1) dx = 2 ln|x+1|\n\n\u222b-1/(x-2) dx = -ln|x-2|\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n3x + ln|x| + 2 ln|x+1| - ln|x-2| + C\n\nBut we might want to combine the logarithmic terms if possible. Let's see:\n\nln|x| + 2 ln|x+1| - ln|x-2| = ln|x| + ln|(x+1)^2| - ln|x-2| = ln[ |x|*(x+1)^2 / |x-2| ]\n\nBut depending on the convention, sometimes it's left as separate terms. So the answer can be written as:\n\n3x + ln|x| + 2 ln|x+1| - ln|x-2| + C\n\nOr if combined, as:\n\n3x + ln| x(x+1)^2 / (x-2) | + C\n\nBut both forms are correct. The problem says \"indefinite integral,\" so either form should be acceptable. However, perhaps the original problem expects the combined form? Let me check if there are simplifications or if constants can be absorbed into C.\n\nWait, no, when combining logs, multiplicative constants become exponents, so that's already done. So the answer can be presented either way, but since the partial fractions are split into separate terms, integrating them would result in separate log terms. However, combining them into a single logarithm is also acceptable. Let me check if my partial fractions were correctly calculated.\n\nLet me verify the decomposition. We had:\n\nOriginal expression: (2x\u00b2 -6x -2)/[x(x+1)(x-2)] = 1/x + 2/(x+1) -1/(x-2)\n\nLet me combine the right-hand side:\n\n1/x + 2/(x+1) -1/(x-2) = [ (x+1)(x-2) + 2x(x-2) -x(x+1) ] / [x(x+1)(x-2)]\n\nWait, perhaps an easier way to verify is to plug in specific x values.\n\nLet me test x=0: Left-hand side: (0 -0 -2)/(0*(1)*(-2)) but x=0 is a root of the denominator, so undefined. Let's use another value. Let's pick x=1 (not a root of denominator):\n\nLeft-hand side: (2*1 -6*1 -2)/(1*2*(-1)) = (2 -6 -2)/( -