ng y): (\u00b11, 0, \u00b11) \u2192 4 points.\n\n- For edges along z-axis (changing z): (\u00b11, \u00b11, 0) \u2192 4 points.\n\nSo all midpoints have exactly one coordinate as 0 and the other two as \u00b11. Therefore, a midpoint cannot have a coordinate of 1 and -1 in different positions. So (1,-1,1) is actually a vertex of the cube, not a midpoint. Oops, my mistake earlier. So M2 cannot be (1,-1,1). Let's correct that.\n\nLet me pick two midpoints that are on skew edges. For example, take M1 = (1,1,0) (midpoint of a z-axis edge) and M2 = (0,1,1) (midpoint of an x-axis edge). Are these connected by a skew line? Let's see. The line connecting (1,1,0) and (0,1,1). This line goes from (1,1,0) to (0,1,1). Is this line skew with respect to any edge? Not sure, but perhaps the segment between them is not along an edge, face, or space diagonal. Let's compute the perpendicular bisector plane.\n\nMidpoint is ((1+0)/2, (1+1)/2, (0+1)/2) = (0.5, 1, 0.5). The vector from M1 to M2 is (-1,0,1). So the normal vector to the plane is (-1,0,1). The equation of the plane is -1*(x - 0.5) + 0*(y - 1) + 1*(z - 0.5) = 0 \u2192 -x + 0.5 + z - 0.5 = 0 \u2192 -x + z = 0 \u2192 z = x.\n\nNow, check which midpoints lie on the plane z = x. Midpoints are:\n\n- (0, \u00b11, \u00b11): z = x \u21d2 0 = \u00b11? No.\n\n- (\u00b11, 0, \u00b11): z = x \u21d2 \u00b11 = \u00b11. So if x = 1, z must be 1; if x = -1, z must be -1. So (1,0,1) and (-1,0,-1).\n\n- (\u00b11, \u00b11, 0): z = x \u21d2 0 = \u00b11? No.\n\nSo the plane z = x contains the midpoints (1,0,1) and (-1,0,-1). These are two midpoints, different from M1 = (1,1,0) and M2 = (0,1,1). Therefore, the perpendicular bisector plane passes through two other midpoints. So this case also satisfies the condition.\n\nCase 4: M1 and M2 are midpoints connected by a space diagonal.\n\nWait, but in the cube, the space diagonals go from vertex to opposite vertex. The midpoints are on edges, so the segment connecting two midpoints can't be a space diago