r plane would be invariant under some of these symmetries, leading to other midpoints lying on it.\n\nAlternatively, considering that the perpendicular bisector plane of two midpoints must pass through the origin, and it's defined by equidistant points. Since all midpoints are at distance sqrt(2) from the origin, but the plane equation would impose a linear condition that some other midpoints satisfy.\n\nAnother approach: Given two midpoints M1 and M2, the perpendicular bisector plane equation is (M1 - M2) \u00b7 (X - (M1 + M2)/2) = 0. Since the origin is equidistant to M1 and M2, it lies on the plane, so substituting X=0 gives (M1 - M2) \u00b7 (- (M1 + M2)/2) = 0 \u2192 -(M1 - M2) \u00b7 (M1 + M2)/2 =0 \u2192 -( |M1|\u00b2 - |M2|\u00b2 ) /2 =0. But since |M1|\u00b2 = |M2|\u00b2 = 1\u00b2 +1\u00b2 +0\u00b2=2, so this is zero. Hence, origin is on the plane.\n\nBut we need to find other midpoints on this plane. Let\u2019s denote the plane equation as (M1 - M2) \u00b7 X = (M1 - M2) \u00b7 (M1 + M2)/2. Since |M1|\u00b2 = |M2|\u00b2, the right-hand side is ( |M1|\u00b2 - |M2|\u00b2 ) /2 =0. So the plane equation simplifies to (M1 - M2) \u00b7 X = 0. So any midpoint X that satisfies (M1 - M2) \u00b7 X =0 lies on the plane.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to showing that there are at least two midpoints X (other than M1 and M2) such that (M1 - M2) \u00b7 X =0.\n\nSince all midpoints have coordinates with two \u00b11 and one 0, we can analyze this dot product.\n\nLet\u2019s denote M1 = (a, b, c) and M2 = (d, e, f), where each of M1 and M2 has two coordinates \u00b11 and one 0. Then (M1 - M2) = (a - d, b - e, c - f).\n\nThe dot product with another midpoint X = (x, y, z) is (a - d)x + (b - e)y + (c - f)z =0.\n\nWe need to find at least two such X (excluding M1 and M2) that satisfy this equation.\n\nLet\u2019s consider different scenarios based on the positions of M1 and M2.\n\nScenario 1: M1 and M2 are on the same axis (but different edges). For example, both are midpoints